1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:04,376
Let’s build a Digital Brain from three health insurance documents.

2
00:00:04,376 --> 00:00:08,553
We upload a Policy System note, a Claims Processing note,

3
00:00:08,553 --> 00:00:12,729
and a Claims Guide. The first explains where policy details

4
00:00:12,729 --> 00:00:16,906
come from, the second describes claim records, and the third

5
00:00:16,906 --> 00:00:21,082
explains the portal. We’ll follow their actual sentences into the

6
00:00:21,082 --> 00:00:21,500
graph.

7
00:00:22,200 --> 00:00:26,700
The application checks access and file integrity. MarkItDown converts the

8
00:00:26,700 --> 00:00:31,200
PDF and Word files into readable text. Here, each document

9
00:00:31,200 --> 00:00:35,700
becomes an Overview heading followed by its sentence. Django stores

10
00:00:35,700 --> 00:00:40,200
this text as a knowledge source. A source identifier tells

11
00:00:40,200 --> 00:00:44,700
us where it came from; a SHA-256 fingerprint helps detect

12
00:00:44,700 --> 00:00:46,500
whether the content changes.

13
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:51,602
Now we request graph generation. First, our Python builder creates

14
00:00:51,602 --> 00:00:56,005
a node for each document. A node simply means an

15
00:00:56,005 --> 00:01:00,407
item in the graph. It also creates section nodes from

16
00:01:00,407 --> 00:01:04,809
headings and ordinary text lines. Watch the Policy System document

17
00:01:04,809 --> 00:01:09,211
connect to its Overview heading and its source sentence. These

18
00:01:09,211 --> 00:01:13,614
arrows say ‘contains section.’ The other documents follow the same

19
00:01:13,614 --> 00:01:18,016
pattern. Tables, when present, produce records and field values. At

20
00:01:18,016 --> 00:01:22,418
this stage, we have captured document structure; we have not

21
00:01:22,418 --> 00:01:25,500
inferred which business system uses which database.

22
00:01:26,200 --> 00:01:31,011
With optional AI enrichment enabled, OpenAI or Claude reads the

23
00:01:31,011 --> 00:01:35,822
text and proposes business relationships. Let’s examine the first sentence.

24
00:01:35,822 --> 00:01:40,633
‘Claims portal’ is one item, ‘policy database’ is another, and

25
00:01:40,633 --> 00:01:45,444
‘uses’ connects them. We display that as two nodes joined

26
00:01:45,444 --> 00:01:50,256
by an arrow. The proposal also identifies their types, the

27
00:01:50,256 --> 00:01:55,067
source document and the exact quote. The second document proposes

28
00:01:55,067 --> 00:01:59,878
another uses connection, this time to Claims Database. The third

29
00:01:59,878 --> 00:02:04,689
proposes Claims Guide explains Claims Portal. These are suggestions first.

30
00:02:04,689 --> 00:02:09,500
They become accepted connections only after the application checks them.

31
00:02:10,200 --> 00:02:14,488
Each proposal passes through checks. Does the source exist? Is

32
00:02:14,488 --> 00:02:18,776
the exact quote present? Are the named items in it?

33
00:02:18,776 --> 00:02:23,064
Are their types and relationship allowed? Our valid examples pass.

34
00:02:23,064 --> 00:02:27,353
An invented sentence saying the portal uses a Payment Database

35
00:02:27,353 --> 00:02:31,641
fails because that quote is absent. These checks filter unsupported

36
00:02:31,641 --> 00:02:35,500
proposals; they do not replace human review of meaning.

37
00:02:36,200 --> 00:02:40,376
The three proposals mention the same Claims Portal. Matching names

38
00:02:40,376 --> 00:02:44,553
and types become one shared node. Now the business view

39
00:02:44,553 --> 00:02:48,729
has four nodes and three connections. Select an arrow, and

40
00:02:48,729 --> 00:02:52,906
its original document and supporting quote can explain why it

41
00:02:52,906 --> 00:02:57,082
exists. The full graph also retains document structure and evidence

42
00:02:57,082 --> 00:02:57,500
links.

43
00:02:58,200 --> 00:03:02,596
When search embeddings are enabled for this requested AI run,

44
00:03:02,596 --> 00:03:06,992
OpenAI converts relationship text and document passages into numerical vectors.

45
00:03:06,992 --> 00:03:11,389
Django stores them for later meaning-based search. The vectors help

46
00:03:11,389 --> 00:03:15,785
find information when a question is asked; they do not

47
00:03:15,785 --> 00:03:20,181
create or prove the graph connections. Unchanged text can reuse

48
00:03:20,181 --> 00:03:21,500
its stored vector.

49
00:03:22,200 --> 00:03:26,836
Django saves a numbered draft. For review, Graphify uses NetworkX

50
00:03:26,836 --> 00:03:31,473
and Leiden clustering to organise existing connected nodes into themes.

51
00:03:31,473 --> 00:03:36,109
It groups the graph we built; it does not invent

52
00:03:36,109 --> 00:03:37,500
the underlying facts.

53
00:03:38,200 --> 00:03:42,933
A reviewer checks the draft and publishes it. Restricted sources

54
00:03:42,933 --> 00:03:47,667
are excluded from graph generation. Later, graph answers check current

55
00:03:47,667 --> 00:03:52,400
evidence and user access again. We have turned three documents

56
00:03:52,400 --> 00:03:57,133
into connected knowledge, with proof behind the connections. Next, we’ll

57
00:03:57,133 --> 00:03:59,500
see how retrieval uses it.
